---
title: React library
description: The GateKeeper React package — a public SPA client with a provider, hooks, and auth components.
section: Application libraries
---

`@gatekeeper/react` is the React integration for GateKeeper. It wraps the framework-agnostic
`@gatekeeper/spa-oauth` public client in a React provider, hooks, and components, so a React SPA can
sign users in with Authorization Code + PKCE without hand-rolling the flow.

The package source lives at `packages/gatekeeper-react`; a runnable example is at `frontends/react`.

## Requires a public OAuth client

This runs entirely in the browser, so it must use a **public** OAuth client:

- `client_type: "public"` — **no client secret**.
- PKCE S256 is the only client protection.
- Tokens live in `sessionStorage` and are exposed to XSS by design.
- The SPA calls `/oauth/token` and `/oauth/userinfo` directly, so its origin must be in the
  server's `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`.

If your app has a server runtime, prefer a confidential BFF (`@gatekeeper/sveltekit-bff`,
`@gatekeeper/nextjs`, `@gatekeeper/remix`) instead.

## Wrap the app with the provider

```tsx
import { GateKeeperProvider, type GateKeeperSpaConfig } from '@gatekeeper/react';

const config: GateKeeperSpaConfig = {
	idpBaseUrl: import.meta.env.VITE_GATEKEEPER_IDP_BASE_URL,
	authUiBaseUrl: import.meta.env.VITE_GATEKEEPER_AUTH_UI_BASE_URL,
	tenantId: import.meta.env.VITE_GATEKEEPER_TENANT_ID,
	clientId: import.meta.env.VITE_GATEKEEPER_CLIENT_ID,
	redirectUri: `${window.location.origin}/`,
	scope: 'openid profile email'
};

createRoot(document.getElementById('root')!).render(
	<GateKeeperProvider config={config}>
		<App />
	</GateKeeperProvider>
);
```

There is no client secret in this config — only the public `clientId`. The provider builds the
client, subscribes to it with `useSyncExternalStore`, and (by default) finishes the redirect on
mount when the URL carries `?code=`. Pass `handleRedirectCallback={false}` to disable that, or pass
a pre-built `client` instead of `config`.

## Read auth state with hooks

```tsx
import { useGateKeeper, useUser } from '@gatekeeper/react';

function Profile() {
	const { isAuthenticated, user, expiresAt, error, signIn, signOut, refresh, getAccessToken } =
		useGateKeeper();

	if (!isAuthenticated) return <button onClick={() => void signIn()}>Sign in</button>;
	return <p>Signed in as {user?.email ?? user?.sub}</p>;
}
```

`useGateKeeper()` returns `status`, `isAuthenticated`, `isLoading`, `user`, `expiresAt`, `error`,
plus `signIn`, `signOut`, `refresh`, `getAccessToken`, and the raw `client`. `useUser()` is a
shortcut for the current profile.

`getAccessToken()` returns a non-expired access token (refreshing silently if it is within the skew
window), for calling your own APIs.

## Components

```tsx
import { SignInButton, SignOutButton, Authenticated } from '@gatekeeper/react';

<SignInButton>Sign in with GateKeeper</SignInButton>
<Authenticated fallback={<p>Please sign in.</p>}>
	<SignOutButton />
</Authenticated>
```

`<SignInButton>` calls `signIn()` (redirect to the hosted login); `<SignOutButton>` clears local
state; `<Authenticated>` renders its children only when a session exists, with an optional
`fallback`.

## Sign-in / sign-out flow

1. **Sign in** — `signIn()` mints PKCE, stashes the transaction in `sessionStorage`, and redirects
   to `{AUTH_UI_BASE_URL}/auth/{clientId}/sign-in?...`.
2. **Callback** — on return, the provider exchanges `?code=` for tokens and strips the params.
3. **Refresh** — `getAccessToken()`/`refresh()` rotate the refresh token before expiry.
4. **Sign out** — clears `sessionStorage` (there is no server-side logout endpoint).

## Fetch userinfo

```ts
const claims = await client.fetchUserInfo(); // GET /oauth/userinfo?tenant_id=... (Bearer)
```

## Example app

`frontends/react` is a Vite + React 18 SPA (dev port 5175). Register a public client with
`frontends/react/scripts/register-public-client.sh`, paste the output into `.env`, add
`http://localhost:5175` to `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`, then `npm install && npm run dev`.
